# Line endings: LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# The repository half was never the problem — every blob here is already LF. The
# working tree was, because core.autocrlf=true rewrites files to CRLF on checkout
# while files written by other tools stay LF, leaving a checkout half one and half
# the other. That is not cosmetic. It cost us a broken release once: a patch script
# matched multi-line anchors with \n against a CRLF working tree, so only its
# single-line edits applied and TraceIt.php was left passing an argument its
# signature did not declare — lint-clean, broken at runtime (218582f).
#
# eol=lf rather than a bare text=auto, so the working tree is LF for everyone.
# Any grep, sed or regex that assumes \n now behaves the same on every machine,
# and PHP on the client's Linux servers sees exactly what is committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touch these: newline conversion corrupts them.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# Better hunk headers when diffing PHP: git names the function a change is in.
*.php diff=php
*.md  diff=markdown
